      "content": "Critics of high-volume immigration often argue that immediate increases in government spending for public services, such as healthcare and education, could outweigh the long-term economic contributions of new arrivals. They also frequently point to the potential strain on local infrastructure and the costs associated with housing shortages and increased demand on public facilities. While these are valid points of debate, major economists generally note that workers typically generate more economic activity than they consume once they become established. Understanding these specific fiscal tensions helps explain the controversy without needing to jump to conclusions about national collapse.",
